Section 6: Responsible AI Requirements 

Human Oversight 
* Every AI-assisted code change requires at least one human peer reviewer before it can merge into a protected branch (COP-07).
* Developers are designated as the accountable party for any suggestion they accept, reinforced through signed policy acknowledgment (COP-06).
* Engineering Managers and Tech Leads are expected to treat CoPilot as an accelerant to engineering judgement, not a replacement for it.

Transparency
* All developers complete AI literacy training before seat activation, covering CoPilot's capabilities and limitations (COP-11).
* Material platform updates, new features, or policy changes are communicated to all users at least five business days in advance (COP-15).
* An internal AI usage policy documents where and how CoPilot is used across the organization.

Accountability
* Individual developers are accountable for code they accept and commit, regardless of AI involement (COP-06).
* Engineering Managers and Tech Leads own enforement of mandatory review within their teams.
* The AI Governance Lead owns the overall control framework; the CEO and Executive Leadership hold ultimate reputational and organizational accountability for AI-related incidents.

Fairness 
* Quarterly sampling of CoPilot suggestions assesses bias in generated code patterns, in addition to quality and security adherence (COP-10).
* Findings are reviewed by the AI Governance Committee, which can recommend usage restrictions or additional controls where patterns of concern emerge.

